  "title": "Chapter 48: Budget and Taxation in India",
  "order": 48,
  "blocks": [
    {
      "id": "gd9lsqt",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "1. Meaning of Budget"
    },
    {
      "type": "keyFacts",
      "title": "Key Takeaways",
      "items": [
        "The Constitution of India does not use the word 'Budget'; it specifies it as the 'Annual Financial Statement' under Article 112.",
        "The financial year in India runs from April 1 of the current year to March 31 of the following year.",
        "Budget receipts are split into Revenue Receipts (non-liability creating) and Capital Receipts (liability creating or asset reducing).",
        "Direct taxes are non-transferable and progressive, whereas Indirect taxes are shiftable and regressive in nature.",
        "Fiscal Deficit indicates the total borrowing requirements of the Government of India during a financial year."
      ],
      "id": "7ml89fl"
    },
    {
      "type": "statStrip",
      "stats": [
        {
          "label": "Constitutional Provision",
          "value": "Article 112",
          "icon": "book"
        },
        {
          "label": "Financial Year",
          "value": "April 1 - March 31",
          "icon": "calendar"
        },
        {
          "label": "GST Amendment",
          "value": "101st CAA, 2016",
          "icon": "award"
        },
        {
          "label": "Primary Deficit Formula",
          "value": "Fiscal Deficit - Interest",
